    schema:url <virtuoso-ai-value-prop.html#/agents?agent=virtuoso-support&ask=How%20do%20I%20map%20Databricks%20tables%20attached%20to%20Virtuoso%20with%20R2RML%3F> .

<#faq-1> a schema:Question ;
    schema:name "Does Virtuoso generate an ontology from my database?"@en ;
    schema:acceptedAnswer <#faq-1-answer> ;
    schema:isPartOf <#section-questions> .

<#faq-1-answer> a schema:Answer ;
    schema:text "Yes. Generating an RDF View also generates its ontology: a class for every table, a typed datatype property for every column, and an object property for every foreign key, each with its own IRI and a label naming the SQL source. You can then align those classes with shared ontologies such as FOAF, schema.org or DBpedia, and query every source in the shared terms. The Ontologies page shows both on the live server."@en .

<#faq-2> a schema:Question ;
    schema:name "Which databases does this work with?"@en ;
    schema:acceptedAnswer <#faq-2-answer> ;
    schema:isPartOf <#section-questions> .

<#faq-2-answer> a schema:Answer ;
    schema:text "Any DBMS that Virtuoso can reach over ODBC or JDBC. Its tables are linked into Virtuoso and can then be exposed through an RDF View, so every row gets its own hyperlink. The showcases on this site use Microsoft SQL Server, PostgreSQL, Databricks and native Virtuoso SQL tables as examples, plus a synthetic Snowflake-style warehouse estate. The Databricks view is mapped with R2RML, the W3C mapping language, which Virtuoso turns into quad maps."@en .

<#faq-3> a schema:Question ;
    schema:name "What is an RDF View?"@en ;
    schema:acceptedAnswer <#faq-3-answer> ;
    schema:isPartOf <#section-questions> .

<#faq-3-answer> a schema:Answer ;
    schema:text "A mapping, written as quad maps, that presents the tables of any ODBC- or JDBC-accessible DBMS as RDF without copying them. Each row gets a hyperlink of its own that works as a cross-platform super-key, columns become properties and foreign keys become links. SPARQL queries are translated to SQL against the source tables at query time."@en .

<#faq-4> a schema:Question ;
    schema:name "What does SQL over XMLA mean here?"@en ;
    schema:acceptedAnswer <#faq-4-answer> ;
    schema:isPartOf <#section-questions> .

<#faq-4-answer> a schema:Answer ;
    schema:text "XMLA (XML for Analysis) is a SOAP protocol for sending SQL to a server over HTTP. The SPASQL Query Builder posts your SQL to Virtuoso's /XMLA endpoint with the data source DSN=Local_Instance, so a browser can run SQL without an ODBC driver. Each SQL card can show the request it sends."@en .

<#faq-5> a schema:Question ;
    schema:name "What is SPASQL?"@en ;
    schema:acceptedAnswer <#faq-5-answer> ;
    schema:isPartOf <#section-questions> .

<#faq-5-answer> a schema:Answer ;
    schema:text "SPARQL inside SQL. A SPARQL query is placed in a SQL FROM clause, written FROM (SPARQL …) AS alias, and its result behaves like a derived table. Any SQL client can then use graph data, and SQL can filter or join it further."@en .

<#faq-6> a schema:Question ;
    schema:name "Why do SQL and SPASQL ask me to sign in?"@en ;
    schema:acceptedAnswer <#faq-6-answer> ;
    schema:isPartOf <#section-questions> .

<#faq-6-answer> a schema:Answer ;
    schema:text "The demo instance is protected by ABAC ACLs. SPARQL is open to anonymous users, while SQL and SPASQL need a session. Sign in with user name demo and password demo, or vdb and vdb. The Query Builder links on this site already preset the demo account for the query itself."@en .

<#faq-7> a schema:Question ;
    schema:name "What is the Sponger?"@en ;
    schema:acceptedAnswer <#faq-7-answer> ;
    schema:isPartOf <#section-questions> .

<#faq-7-answer> a schema:Answer ;
    schema:text "Virtuoso's Linked Data middleware. Cartridges turn web resources such as HTML pages, feeds, repositories and social posts into RDF, stored in a graph named by the source URL. Adding DEFINE get:soft \"replace\" to a query fetches or refreshes that graph."@en .

<#faq-8> a schema:Question ;
    schema:name "Why do the Sponger cards run without the DEFINE pragma?"@en ;
    schema:acceptedAnswer <#faq-8-answer> ;
    schema:isPartOf <#section-questions> .

<#faq-8-answer> a schema:Answer ;
    schema:text "Fetching new content needs a signed-in session on URIBurner. The graphs the Sponger has already built can be read by anyone, so the cards query those, and the Re-sponge link adds the pragma for a fresh fetch."@en .

<#faq-9> a schema:Question ;
    schema:name "How are OPAL agents “loosely coupled” with Data Spaces?"@en ;
    schema:acceptedAnswer <#faq-9-answer> ;
    schema:isPartOf <#section-questions> .

<#faq-9-answer> a schema:Answer ;
    schema:text "An agent holds instructions and a list of query functions, not a copy of the data. At answer time the model picks a function and Virtuoso runs a predefined SQL, SPARQL or SPASQL query against whichever Data Space the function names. You can change the model, the agent or the data without rebuilding the others."@en .

<#faq-10> a schema:Question ;
    schema:name "Can I put the chat widget on my own site?"@en ;
    schema:acceptedAnswer <#faq-10-answer> ;
    schema:isPartOf <#section-questions> .

<#faq-10-answer> a schema:Answer ;
    schema:text "Yes. Load opal.js and auth.js, then create new Opal(...) with your host, callbacks and an agent module, as the Widget usage section shows. Register your own API key on URIBurner, or sign users in with Solid OIDC."@en .

<#faq-11> a schema:Question ;
    schema:name "What is the faceted browser?"@en ;
    schema:acceptedAnswer <#faq-11-answer> ;
    schema:isPartOf <#section-questions> .

<#faq-11-answer> a schema:Answer ;
    schema:text "A single-page app in the style of Virtuoso's FCT service. It narrows a set of entities by text, type, property and value, pivots along relationships, and keeps the whole view in its URL so any view can be shared."@en .

